WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.940
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: How do I
say Hello, World in Japanese?

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.620
SPEAKER 1: [JAPANESE]

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.050
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And
what about in Spanish?

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:08.238
SPEAKER 2: [SPANISH]

00:00:08.238 --> 00:00:09.780
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, stay with me

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:14.456
as we explore the Translation
API to make our apps global.

00:00:14.456 --> 00:00:17.907
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.660
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:27.020
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.180
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and today, we're

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.850
talking about Translation API.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:35.300
Have you ever typed "how
do I say tomatoes in Hindi"

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.570
in your Google Search?

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:38.650
I bet you have.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:42.580
Well, maybe not exactly that,
but something similar, right?

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:46.090
Then you have used Google's
translation models.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.360
But what if you could use the
power of this translation model

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.390
to translate your
websites and apps, making

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:56.980
them instantly usable across
the world in multiple languages?

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.650
Well, that's where
Translation API comes in.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.800
It is a high-quality
pre-change translation model

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.490
that can automatically identify
more than 100 languages

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:07.930
and translate them.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.510
You can easily integrate
your website or apps

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:12.550
with it using the REST API.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:14.230
Let's see how.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.320
First, enable the Translation
API in your project

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.320
and create a service
account for authentication.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.275
Make sure you provide the
Cloud Translation API editor

00:01:23.275 --> 00:01:27.190
role to your service account
and generate a JSON key.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.400
This will be used to
authenticate our call.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.150
I love Cloud Shells, so here,
I'm using curl from Cloud Shell

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.070
to call the Translation API.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.710
But you can use the
language of your choice.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.600
We authenticate
the service using

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.970
the key we downloaded earlier.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:46.030
Now, let's say we want to
translate "the weather is

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.670
amazing today" into Hindi.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.520
Here, we are setting
both source and target,

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.920
but the source is
optional, since the API can

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.050
autodetect the source language.

00:01:56.050 --> 00:02:00.910
We pass this requester JSON file
as a payload with our HTTP post

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:03.580
request to the Translation API.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:08.350
And we get the
translation, [HINDI]..

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.370
This is great.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.350
Let's try another example now.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:15.820
"Google Cloud is an
amazing technology."

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:19.420
Even though this is technically
a correct translation,

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.490
it still feels weird.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.760
This is because Google
Cloud is a proper noun,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.710
and should not be translated.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.220
In such cases, where you have
product names, company names,

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.400
and other proper nouns,
you would like more control

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.270
over how these specific
words are translated.

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:40.570
That's where Translation
API's Advanced Glossary

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:41.710
feature comes in.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.820
It is essentially
a custom dictionary

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.810
that the API uses to
consistently translate

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:49.750
your domain-specific
terminology.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.020
Here's how it works.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:54.370
We create a glossary file
with samples of our source

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.740
and target language
phrases and save it

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.480
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.310
Then, we make a
translation request

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.590
using the glossary files.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:05.420
Now, let's apply
this to our example.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:09.280
We create a glossary file,
name it glossary.csv.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.800
And you can also create
them in TMX or TSV formats,

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:13.900
if you want.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.940
Let's add some examples here.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.350
Google Cloud stays the same,
Translation API stays the same,

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.720
while technology translates.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.510
Upload this file
to a GCS bucket.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.400
Before we start
using the glossary,

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.820
we need to register it and
create a glossary ID, wherein

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.370
the payload, we mention
the source and target

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.310
language, along with the
location of the file.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:38.260
And now, we are
ready to translate

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.990
the text using our glossary.

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:44.020
And here we see the translation
now takes our glossary

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:48.100
into account and considers
Google Cloud as a proper noun.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.350
So far, we've seen
text translation

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:51.710
for a single string.

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.340
But what if you wanted to
translate multiple files

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.140
in multiple languages?

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.170
That's where the Batch
Translation feature comes in.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:00.190
It is easy.

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.920
Upload the files you want to
translate into Google Cloud

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:06.880
Storage, request translations
in multiple languages,

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.490
and then store the translations
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.430
Let's see how this works.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:15.460
I have this sample text
file that we will translate

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:17.470
in a few different languages.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:21.769
We define our request body in
the three languages, Spanish,

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:23.410
French, and Hindi.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.890
Provide the path for the source
and destination in Google Cloud

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.920
Storage, then make a
call to the API endpoint

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.760
with Batch Translate text
using our request body.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.040
Now, let's go to our
Google Cloud Storage bucket

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.230
to see our translated files.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.710
And they are available
for us to use.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.620
In this episode, we
learned how Translation API

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.620
works, and tried some advanced
features, such as Batch

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.960
Translation for batch
translation needs

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.180
and Glossary for
correctly translating

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.100
proper notes and product names.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.560
I look forward to seeing
you on the next episode

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.120
of "AI Adventures,"
where we will

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.300
explore custom
domain-specific models using

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.680
AutoML translation.

